WEBVTT

1
00:34:30.160 --> 00:34:35.480
But in lieu of a PowerPoint, I'm going to

2
00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:41.000
I'm going to invite you all
to take a look at my exhibit.

3
00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:44.760
I just posted the link in chat because
I think that's going to be more effective

4
00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:48.000
in outlining
what I'm speaking about than a PowerPoint.

5
00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:52.000
And I promise, it has nothing to do
with the fact that I hate PowerPoints.

6
00:34:52.200 --> 00:34:55.520
So my name is Glenn Ritchey.

7
00:34:55.520 --> 00:35:00.080
I am a going into my second year
in the Texts and Technology program

8
00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:06.080
here at UCF, and 
I generally hate doing the introductions.

9
00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:09.680
So I will say that my work is interested

10
00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:13.440
in electronic literature
and digital fiction broadly,

11
00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:19.200
my background is more in traditional
literary and cultural studies, with

12
00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:21.280
focuses on

13
00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:25.640
publishing and editing
experimental literature.

14
00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:30.520
A lot of my work
likewise hinges on the cinematic.

15
00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:36.000
So my paper is called “Coercing
Contribution: Navigating

16
00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:39.280
Involuntary Authors
through a Mountain of Sleep.”

17
00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:42.520
And before I get going, I'm just going
to say thanks all for being here.

18
00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:43.960
Much appreciated.

19
00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:49.080
And thanks to everyone
who has made EL0 ‘26 happen.

20
00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:52.920
So this paper handles
less of the technical end,

21
00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:58.760
but more of the theoretical
concerns of “Like a Mountaib of Sleep,”

22
00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:03.160
which I'll just be calling “LaMoS.”
It was built in Twine SugarCube

23
00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:06.800
and is structured across
two distinct passage populations

24
00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:10.320
a small cluster of named,
narratively distinct passages.

25
00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:15.160
In essence, narrative beats connected
by explicit links in a much larger set

26
00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:20.320
of structurally undifferentiated passages
that function as a destination pool,

27
00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:24.440
where reader player input
is captured at prompts throughout the text

28
00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:29.280
and is randomized
at the point of reinfection, meaning no

29
00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:33.680
two traversals redeploy identical
input into identical output,

30
00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:37.960
and to give everything away,
the reader receives a free verse poem

31
00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:40.760
for their efforts
at the end of the experience,

32
00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:43.760
assembled from their own words
and returned to them,

33
00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:47.840
perhaps unrecognized
as what I'm calling a gift.

34
00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:52.080
This is a textual project
I've worked on for the better and worse

35
00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:53.680
parts of ten years.

36
00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:58.000
In fact, yesterday I was recalling
Patrick Lichty’s workshop on spatial

37
00:36:58.040 --> 00:37:01.040
texts in XR
game engines to PD [a colleague].

38
00:37:01.120 --> 00:37:03.120
I was mostly talking
about how I looked forward

39
00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:06.440
to exploring yet
another remediation of this project

40
00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:10.440
as it began as a spatial text,
and PD stated, “I love that you have

41
00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:14.680
a foundational written text that you keep
exploring in different forms.”

42
00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:18.560
Of course I'm paraphrasing,
but it left me to think

43
00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:21.840
maybe this has been the better part
of the last ten years.

44
00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:25.000
With that said,
this project began as a reflection

45
00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.080
of my desire to create a filmic homage

46
00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:31.880
to some of my favorite directors
from mid-century continental Europe,

47
00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:36.720
all of whom specialized in the effectively
called Eurotrash.

48
00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:40.760
Really, one gets the idea
if they've seen Umberto Lenzi’s

49
00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:45.120
Welcome to Spring Break from 1989,
also called Nightmare Beach,

50
00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:50.360
which was filmed partially
in my hometown of Daytona Beach, Florida.

51
00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:54.880
I soon learned that making a film,
even on a shoestring budget,

52
00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:58.120
is still a really tall order
that, despite my optimism

53
00:37:58.120 --> 00:38:01.120
and experimentation,
just did not seem feasible.

54
00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:05.400
So I moved on to writing a novel
that would be about 300 pages,

55
00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:09.120
describing the final ten minutes
of this film that would never exist.

56
00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:12.680
I ended up sitting with the opening
few thousand words

57
00:38:12.680 --> 00:38:16.320
for quite some time, as I could
never get this project where I hoped.

58
00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:19.120
Now, as of my more recent forays

59
00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:22.960
into electronic literature
and digital fictions, in my PhD,

60
00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:26.640
I spent a lot of time experimenting
with hypertext forms.

61
00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:31.360
The result of my last year
is this present iteration of “LaMoS,”

62
00:38:31.360 --> 00:38:35.720
which postures
as a VHS of an unrealized slasher

63
00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:40.480
film set in a Florida
that MTV Spring Break forgot.

64
00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:45.160
With all of this said, my goal for
this project is partly a celebration

65
00:38:45.160 --> 00:38:50.040
and interrogation of the affordances
electronic literature offers,

66
00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:55.000
as well as a statement of appreciation
for the Foucauldian author function.

67
00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:59.880
Those areas where we must realize that
our conception of the author is limited

68
00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:03.160
to the sum of a network, in essence,
authors,

69
00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:08.360
publishers, marketers,
and in this case, programmers

70
00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:12.680
and reader players,
amongst many other examples.

71
00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:18.040
I've also landed on how this work yearns
to be a cybertext labyrinth.

72
00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:21.400
Espen Aarseth’s Cybertext

73
00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:25.880
distinguishes the reader of conventional
narrative from the player of a cybertext

74
00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:30.680
on the basis of effort, stating,
“A reader, however strongly engaged in

75
00:39:30.680 --> 00:39:35.600
the unfolding of a narrative, is powerless
like a spectator at a soccer game.

76
00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:39.760
He may speculate, conjecture,
extrapolate, even, shall abuse,

77
00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:45.160
but he's not a player.” Cybertext demands
what Aarseth calls “non-trivial effort

78
00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:49.800
in the form of real traversal,
in real choice, with consequences

79
00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:53.160
as the condition of the reader's
evaluation to player.”

80
00:39:53.320 --> 00:39:56.240
In discussing the labyrinth qualities

81
00:39:56.240 --> 00:40:00.200
applied to literary texts
and in the pursuit of defining cybertext,

82
00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:04.240
he is equally precise about what
a labyrinth requires structurally.

83
00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:07.480
Reviewing critics
who describe strictly linear

84
00:40:07.520 --> 00:40:11.760
print novels as labyrinths without exits,
incidentally citing a longtime

85
00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:15.680
influence of mine, Alain
Robbe-Grillet in his novel Lavoisier.

86
00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:21.760
Among other examples, Aarseth objects
A labyrinth without exit is elaborate

87
00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:25.480
without entrance, in other words,
not a labyrinth at all.

88
00:40:25.760 --> 00:40:27.840
These critics
reached for a spatial metaphor

89
00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:32.040
to describe difficulty
and disorientation, achieved entirely

90
00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:36.960
through prose style, in texts
with no actual branching structure

91
00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:41.720
to navigate beyond that
which is fixed and set before the reader.

92
00:40:42.280 --> 00:40:45.840
The metaphor, Aarseth
insists, was a category error,

93
00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:49.080
and this distinction is something
that led my interests

94
00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:52.720
toward electronic literature
and its various forms, where

95
00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:57.400
most of the literature that I was educated
on, those claiming experimental

96
00:40:57.400 --> 00:41:01.880
or avant garde status, always left me
wanting my frustration.

97
00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:06.520
Developing this project, from filmic
to literate to e-literate, reflects

98
00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:10.160
the desire to establish a looping system
that reflects the horror

99
00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:15.120
element of my narrative
I yanked from Eurotrash films like Lenzi’s

100
00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:19.640
Nightmare City and not Nightmare
Beach and Bernard Launois’s

101
00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:23.920
“Il était une fois le diable.”
The main narrative element

102
00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:28.680
I particularly enjoyed is what
I just called nightmare looping logic.

103
00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:32.440
The films, essentially, and the same way
as they began, with the former

104
00:41:32.480 --> 00:41:36.960
pointing to existential dread,
in the latter pointing toward an emphasis

105
00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:42.240
on the narrative and its specifics
as an encapsulated performance.

106
00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:46.680
My drive was to develop
an experiential frustration and attempt

107
00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:49.880
replicating that nonsense of my inebriated

108
00:41:49.880 --> 00:41:54.000
spring break character
being chased down by a killer

109
00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:58.360
who is probably a ghost, or,
more likely, might not even exist.

110
00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:02.520
Further into the theoretical underpinning
is Foucault's function

111
00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:06.800
of the author— account of the author
function in “What Is an Author?”

112
00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:11.160
Which distinguishes the author function
from the biographical individual,

113
00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:14.720
claiming it is tied to the legal
and institutional systems

114
00:42:14.720 --> 00:42:20.120
that circumscribe, determine,
and articulate the realm of discourses

115
00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:24.920
as it gives rise to a variety of egos
into a series of subjective positions

116
00:42:24.920 --> 00:42:28.680
that individuals of any class
may come to occupy.

117
00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:32.120
Authorship on this account
is not the property of a person,

118
00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:35.800
but a function distributed across
a set of positions

119
00:42:35.800 --> 00:42:39.040
that a given discursive system
makes available.

120
00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:43.400
My intent for “LaMoS” is to concreteness
this distribution

121
00:42:43.400 --> 00:42:47.240
by staging it across four distinct loci.

122
00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:51.440
First, the writer myself,
who produced the original

123
00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:55.560
print pamphlets and prose,
working with Nouveau

124
00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:59.720
Roman influenced constraints of typography
and spatial fragmentation.

125
00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:05.160
Then the designer who built the Twine
remediation and by designer I'm

126
00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:10.240
meaning myself, who did the remediation,
and then those who have built

127
00:43:10.240 --> 00:43:14.280
and maintained twine,
translating the spatial relationships

128
00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:17.720
into navigable nodes,
and writing the rules that govern

129
00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:21.440
the involuntary authorship system itself.

130
00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:25.800
Next, the systems operation at runtime,
capturing input,

131
00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:31.040
applying its transformations,
rejecting text according to the logic

132
00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:36.240
I wrote, but does not directly execute
at the moment of play.

133
00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:39.960
Last is the reader player
who types chooses

134
00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:43.040
and without yet knowing it, contributes
the raw material.

135
00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:46.600
The system will redeploy
as that so-called gift.

136
00:43:46.600 --> 00:43:50.640
It would be difficult
for any of these positions to claim

137
00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:54.600
the resulting poem as it as its own,
as it belongs to the function

138
00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:59.480
that emerges from their interaction
and not to any node in it.

139
00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:03.080
Asked plainly who authored
a given playthrough, the honest answer

140
00:44:03.080 --> 00:44:07.000
to me is all four
and none of them individually.

141
00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:10.200
This point is worth valuing
rather than mourning.

142
00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:14.400
And a more pessimistic
reading of the author

143
00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:18.120
function would treat this distribution
like evidence of loss.

144
00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:22.400
This would take place as the individual
author is dissolved and agency

145
00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:26.760
is conceded to a regulating system
that was more or less

146
00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:31.440
the frequency I operated on in earlier
iterations of this project.

147
00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:35.600
As I've worked more on this project,
my aim is to take this distribution

148
00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:39.760
as a condition
that makes the resulting poem possible.

149
00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:41.400
A poem could not be handed

150
00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:45.840
back to the reader as something
recognizably theirs, but made strange

151
00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:49.800
if authorship were still the property
of a single position.

152
00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:54.800
The multiplication of egos, Foucault
describes, is not merely diagnosed

153
00:44:54.800 --> 00:45:00.400
by “LaMos,” but the work depends on it
to provide the output.

154
00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:03.120
And I'm about five minutes out.

155
00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:07.200
Yep. As stated earlier, “LaMoS”’s
genealogy

156
00:45:07.200 --> 00:45:10.920
runs through cinema and prose
before it runs through cybertext theory.

157
00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:14.880
Its immediate influences
Alain Robbe-Grillet's Nouveau Roman,

158
00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:19.600
Philip Grande’s visual grammar,
and the exploitation adjacent cinema

159
00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:23.680
of Jess Franco, all of which
invite various points of frustration

160
00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:28.440
while pursuing a set aside disorientation
through flattened psychology.

161
00:45:28.480 --> 00:45:32.560
Focus on material,
surface, and spatial fragmentation

162
00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:35.520
achieved through prose
rather than structure.

163
00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:40.880
“LaMoS”’s ambition across its earlier film
and novel incarnations was to recreate

164
00:45:40.880 --> 00:45:42.880
that specific disorientation

165
00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:46.520
before twine or cybertext theory
even entered the picture.

166
00:45:46.960 --> 00:45:51.520
I'm left to consider my frustrated peers
in a “Playable Text” seminar

167
00:45:51.560 --> 00:45:54.560
who traversed in earlier and frankly, much

168
00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:57.600
meaner iteration of this project.

169
00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:01.040
Likewise,
I want to attempt clarifying here through

170
00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:05.240
mentioning Tracy Fullerton
and Matthew Farber's extension of Louise

171
00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:09.680
Rosenblatt’s transactional reading theory
to interactive forms,

172
00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:14.440
Rosenblatt’s, aesthetic reading,
attention to the associations, feelings,

173
00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:19.880
attitudes and ideas that these words and
their reference arouse within the reader

174
00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:24.560
describes a playable text’s function
in a way that feels more illustrative

175
00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:29.000
to my argument
than a purist cybertext vocabulary alone.

176
00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:33.200
In this spirit, I found Shira Chess’s
reference of Bonnie Ruberg

177
00:46:33.440 --> 00:46:38.000
in Play Like a Feminist helpful,
which will sound familiar as Ruberg

178
00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:43.600
states cleverly challenged the premise
that necessitates fun as integral

179
00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:46.600
to game design, and

180
00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:50.560
alternatively explains
that the spirit of no-fun is the spirit

181
00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:54.920
of alternatives, of disruption,
of disruptions or difference.

182
00:46:55.120 --> 00:46:58.080
The pathfinding of feminist
play is illuminating for someone

183
00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:01.400
without a game design background,
or one in games in general.

184
00:47:01.600 --> 00:47:05.000
The outside position renders

185
00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:09.160
“LaMoS” technically a playable text
that happens to satisfy

186
00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:14.760
asks formal criteria for cybertext,
not a project that set out to build one.

187
00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:19.120
Returning for a moment to Chess, I begin
recognizing this projects value,

188
00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:22.040
if only personal value,
through the statement

189
00:47:22.040 --> 00:47:27.160
which pulls from Salen and Zimmerman's
Rules of Play and its implication

190
00:47:27.160 --> 00:47:30.800
that in order to play,
you need rules to push back out

191
00:47:30.800 --> 00:47:34.400
to be part of a larger,
functional, playful mechanism.

192
00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:39.360
And with that, I immediately emailed
doctor Mike Shier exclaiming, “Ha ha!

193
00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:43.280
You should have failed me in this course
because my text was not playable.”

194
00:47:43.280 --> 00:47:45.720
But then I realized that this reflects

195
00:47:45.720 --> 00:47:49.600
a deeper articulation
of my enjoyment of both ludic narratives

196
00:47:49.600 --> 00:47:55.800
and my own proclivity that to win could be
and is probably unnecessary.

197
00:47:55.840 --> 00:48:00.000
So this paper’s political dimension
lives in the vocabulary

198
00:48:00.960 --> 00:48:04.960
used throughout: “usurped,” “coercing,”
“voluntary.”

199
00:48:05.080 --> 00:48:08.920
It is also made clear in Foucault's
own account of the author function

200
00:48:08.920 --> 00:48:12.320
as tied to institutional
and discursive regulation.

201
00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:16.680
These aspects reflect the genre 
I am working under as much as any single

202
00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:17.400
theorist.

203
00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:20.440
Robbe-Grillet, Franco, and Lenzi,
among others.

204
00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:25.120
But worth mentioning now is that works
by Claire Denis and Catherine Breillat

205
00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:30.320
have equally shaped this project since its
earliest unrealized film incarnation.

206
00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:34.960
My present work on the differentials
between Giallo and slasher cinema

207
00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:37.960
and rendering them
through generative literature pipelines

208
00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:42.000
read through questions
of sexism and feminism, continues

209
00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:46.320
along the same trajectory “LaMoS”
has been on for nearly ten years, even

210
00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:48.360
for a conference paper of this length.

211
00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:52.160
I do not presently have room to develop
the political register into a sustained

212
00:48:52.160 --> 00:48:55.200
argument, because there is much
I'm still trying to articulate.

213
00:48:55.240 --> 00:48:58.880
Still, I wanted to name it here
rather than let it pass silently.

214
00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:02.520
And with that said, thank you for your
patience and time, everybody.
